Subject: keep long-path Unix sockets at their real path

"docker exec pg psql -U postgres" failed in ThothDock with "connection to
server on socket /var/run/postgresql/.s.PGSQL.5432 failed: No such file or
directory", while TCP worked. A container's rootfs is deep, so the host path
of a socket in it does not fit sun_path (108 bytes). PRoot then bound the
socket at a random short name and remembered the guest path in a binding of
that one PRoot: "docker exec" (another PRoot), a second container sharing
the volume and test -S never found it, and a connect from another PRoot
made up a new random name that did not exist.

Such a socket now gets a deterministic short name in PROOT_TMP_DIR,
prootsock-<FNV-1a of the host path>:

- bind refuses an existing address with EADDRINUSE (like the kernel), binds
  at the short name, and at the syscall's exit renames the socket to its
  real path and leaves the short name as a symlink to it; if the rename
  fails, the old per-PRoot binding is kept;
- connect goes through the short name, (re)made a symlink to the real path
  by whichever PRoot needs it;
- getsockname, getpeername and accept read the kernel's short name back
  through the symlink, then translate to the guest path;
- symlinks whose socket is gone are removed at the next such bind.

Only binds of long paths add a ptrace stop (at their exit); nothing else
changes. 32-bit socketcall keeps the old behaviour.

Upstream: termux/proot @ 7266fb3e8516535682f5a9c8f3a7e70f6506eddb (v5.1.107.92), after patches 0001-0013

